                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9cbb928c-570e-3d5c-8f67-7168b9ad05f0",
                        "entry_id": "7aedd5f5-e1aa-3711-b874-cb8fb18be257",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of Cocama."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "61783b13-907d-427e-a928-440b73bd8755",
                                "sense_id": "9cbb928c-570e-3d5c-8f67-7168b9ad05f0",
                                "text": "The Kokamas looked disturbed when they arrived in Shoshone."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1c14721e-42b6-456a-8804-e0be9f72f431",
                                "sense_id": "9cbb928c-570e-3d5c-8f67-7168b9ad05f0",
                                "text": "Simultaneously, the Kokama Indians of the lower Ucayali region began to emigrate to the Brazilian Solim5es, where they established themselves among the Tukuna, though in separate localities."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4a9f0bd2-bef1-4ae6-96f1-2ddd3a85b506",
                                "sense_id": "9cbb928c-570e-3d5c-8f67-7168b9ad05f0",
                                "text": "Espírito Santo (Amazonas state), with an area of 33,849 has. for a population of 121 inhabitants, permanently occupied by the Kokama people."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "88608594-7f6d-40b0-b04a-b0e52e1da319",
                                "sense_id": "9cbb928c-570e-3d5c-8f67-7168b9ad05f0",
                                "text": "Influencing other ethnic groups and beyond The Ticuna project has already expanded to include teachers from two other ethnic groups, Kokama and Kaixana."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                "word": "kokama",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "8c8bd94d-85bc-4ce5-a4c5-cc486e2820b9",
                        "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "kokamas"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a89db619-58bb-3099-9fa8-1fecfeae654d",
                        "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A gemsbok."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2a4c538f-9722-4fd9-b20d-39e203a824ee",
                                "sense_id": "a89db619-58bb-3099-9fa8-1fecfeae654d",
                                "text": "I have heard that the Matabili have no name for this animal; but the Bechuana term is \"Kokama.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "82bcf605-d496-420c-8a8b-2bc26e8857b9",
                                "sense_id": "a89db619-58bb-3099-9fa8-1fecfeae654d",
                                "text": "The Oryx capensis of South Africa, or Gemsbok of the Dutch colonists, Kokama of the Bechuanas, is even more striking in its coloring."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "45efb0be-4816-4c11-9404-66b4bd2d29b8",
                                "sense_id": "a89db619-58bb-3099-9fa8-1fecfeae654d",
                                "text": "The Gemsbok (Oryx capensis) is sometimes called the kokama, is nearly four feet in height, and has South Africa as its habitat."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7f3e711d-cb2f-344a-8b35-2fd489345ca9",
                        "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A mangosteen."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "India"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4d911355-166d-408e-a0d2-6e53c776bb8b",
                                "sense_id": "7f3e711d-cb2f-344a-8b35-2fd489345ca9",
                                "text": "Kokama or amsul is the pulp of the fruit cleared of the seeds and dried in the sun and slightly salted. It is of a black colour and an oval shape. The epidermis is wrinkled. At the base of kokama the calyx and the remainder of the stalk are often seen."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3553436a-56f9-43d0-8062-b42a836ecdec",
                                "sense_id": "7f3e711d-cb2f-344a-8b35-2fd489345ca9",
                                "text": "Kokum, Ratdmbi, the fruit kokama, amasula, brinddo, Bom. ; Brindao, Goa."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c16cf5a6-15d7-32e9-9410-4e1054f30873",
                        "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A variety of corn with purple kernels cultivated by the Hopi."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fdeae4b0-93bb-48f7-aa90-1e0d01a0d052",
                                "sense_id": "c16cf5a6-15d7-32e9-9410-4e1054f30873",
                                "text": "In addition, black (or purple) corn, known as kokoma, or Masau'u's corn, symbolically representing the direction of Above, is planted in May for the fall havest."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5a86ed25-6ba9-4642-81d3-cce83202ad9f",
                                "sense_id": "c16cf5a6-15d7-32e9-9410-4e1054f30873",
                                "text": "Contrast of first and most recent regenerations of USDA Hopi kokoma and blue maize varieties conserved ex situ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "42a33102-0458-4d41-a375-49bb6733bc0a",
                                "sense_id": "c16cf5a6-15d7-32e9-9410-4e1054f30873",
                                "text": "Among just five families, Whiting found the following grops were still grown on a regular basis: yellow, red, blue, white, violet, pink, and speckled flour and flint corns; purple-backed kokama corn; sweet corn; shite and gray lima beans; white and blue string beans; grease beans; pole beans; white tepary beans; peanuts; watermelons; casaba melons; honeydew melons; muskmelons; banana squash; cushaw squash; Hubbard squash; cucumbers; onions; chilies; tomatoes; turnips; red dye amaranths; cabbages; peaches; pears; apricots; apples; grapes; and cherries."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "236bc3fa-3bba-3020-b708-8c9dd063ed98",
                        "entry_id": "aaae0bfb-778e-33de-bf2d-20a38c696182",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A variety of grass Rottboellia cochinchinensis."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "809b9a6f-89c9-49c5-bf87-b1847169a814",
                                "sense_id": "236bc3fa-3bba-3020-b708-8c9dd063ed98",
                                "text": "Kokoma or guinea grass (Rottboelia exaltata) is a heavy cropping plant which makes good silage."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "18ce055e-8cdd-4d75-b83a-5e5d05a40d49",
                                "sense_id": "236bc3fa-3bba-3020-b708-8c9dd063ed98",
                                "text": "However, 10 of the 18 places are filled by grass species: 2 Cynodon dactylon (couch grass), 2 Echinochloa crus-galli (barnyard grass), 4 Echinochloa colona (awnless barnyard grass(, 5 Eleusine indica (crowsfoot grass), 6 Sorghum halepense (Johnson grass(, 7 Imperata cylindricata (blady grass), 11 Digitaria sanguinalis (summer grass), 13 Avena fatua (wild oat), 17 Paspalum conjugatum (sourgrass) and 18 Rottboellia cochinchinensis (kokoma grass)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
